pr_comments:
Good catch! Thanks for looking at this.

The rationale you gave here does make sense to me -- the prior state isn't necessarily the same schema as the new state, in the presence of schema upgrades -- but there _is_ a practical implication here that any attribute of type `any` (dynamic pseudo-type) will appear in the output as its raw form with the explicit type information inline:

```
{
  "example": {
    "type": "string",
    "value": "hello"
  }
}
```

The "breaking change"-related impact of this is likely low because there are very few providers today making use of that mechanism, but I know of at least two that seem like they _would_ see the `jsonstate` output change here: `hashicorp/kubernetes-alpha` (the `manifest` argument) and `apparentlymart/testing` (the test assertion values). The latter of these is not so important because it's only data resources anyway, but the `kubernetes-alpha` one could be pretty jarring in how different the output looks before and after.

I don't see any alternative to making this change because as you've noted the current behavior is incorrect and there is no "prior schema" to use instead, but this does seem like something where we ought to do some broadcast communication about it so folks can have a better chance of seeing it coming. The dynamic type serialization also feels like something that would be surprising to an unprepared consumer of the format and thus worth documenting explicitly in [our JSON formats documentation page](https://www.terraform.io/docs/internals/json-format.html).
